1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A running time is not a number of seconds. It is a function of n, the size of the input.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Here are two algorithms on the same problem: one does about 5n steps, the other about n squared.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Below n = 5 the quadratic is the cheaper one. Above n = 5 it is never cheaper again.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
So the question is never "which is faster" but "which grows slower", and that question has a precise answer.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Write f(n) for the work your algorithm does. f is O of g when g is eventually an upper bound up to a constant.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Formally: there is a c greater than zero and an n0 such that for every n at least n0, f(n) is at most c times g(n).

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Take f(n) = 3n squared + 5n + 2 and g(n) = n squared. The witness is c = 4, n0 = 6.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The shaded tail is the whole claim: from n0 onward, f never rises above the dashed ceiling.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Turn the inequality around and you have Omega: g is eventually a lower bound on f, again up to a constant.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Theta needs both: f is Theta of g when f is O of g and f is Omega of g.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
So 3n squared + 5n + 2 is Theta of n squared, and also O of n cubed, but it is not Theta of n cubed.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Write Theta when you can, and say which quantity you are bounding: time, space, or the number of a named operation.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Big-O allows a constant factor to be as large as you like. Little-o forbids equality in the limit.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Take the limit of f(n) over g(n) as n grows. Zero means little-o; a finite positive number means Theta; infinity means little-omega.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The limit is often easiest to read after taking logarithms of both functions, because a product becomes a sum.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
And a sample of values is not a proof: n log n over n to the power 1.01 is still rising at n equal to 10 to the 12.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Nine functions cover almost every answer GATE asks for, and they have one fixed order.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Polynomials are ordered by degree, and any power of n loses to any exponential, whatever the constant.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Two things look like they should matter and do not: the base of a logarithm, and a constant multiplier.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Log of n factorial sits between half n log n and n log n, so sorting by comparison is Theta(n log n).

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Here is a function that is neither O nor Omega of n: g(n) is n to the power 1 plus sine of n.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Take the ratio n over g(n). It equals n to the power minus sine of n, which swings both above and below 1.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
So neither n is O of g, nor g is O of n. Two functions need not be comparable at all.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The same lesson in a friendlier form: f(n squared) is not always Theta of f(n) squared.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Linear search looks at the cells one at a time until it finds the key. The count depends on where the key is.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Best case: the key is first, so one comparison. Worst case: the key is last, so n comparisons.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Average case needs a distribution. Assume the key is equally likely to be in any position, and the average is (n + 1) over 2.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Whatever the distribution, the average lies between the best and the worst. That is the one free fact.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A divide-and-conquer algorithm splits the input and pays for the split. That is a recurrence: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Draw the calls as a tree. Level zero costs n, level one has two nodes costing n over 2 each, so it also costs n.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
There are log base 2 of n plus one levels, each costing n, so the total is n log n. That is the whole solution, with no theorem needed.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The three cases compare the root cost with the leaf count. Whichever is bigger wins; when they tie, the logarithm appears.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Space complexity counts memory cells as a function of n, and the input usually does not count.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
A recursive algorithm pays for its stack. Merge sort runs to depth log n, so its stack is log n frames, not n.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Merge sort needs an n-cell temporary array, so its auxiliary space is n. Heapsort needs a constant number of cells.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
So a space answer has three parts: what is counted, whether the input is included, and the depth rather than the call count.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Four questions answer almost every complexity item. First: is the question about a bound from above, from below, or both?

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Second: is every exponent constant? If not, the polynomial rules and the ladder do not apply.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Third: which quantity, time or space, and whose? The algorithm, the input size, or the depth of the recursion.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Fourth: does the question name an assumption? A distribution, a sorted array, a heap. The answer changes when it does.

